### scan, actor, actorMut

`scan` threads an accumulator, and `actor` and `actorMut` thread the actor state. In every case the call made for one element can observe what the call made for an earlier element left behind, so the calls form a sequence, and the only option is pipeline/nodes.
However, long chains of stages may require compositions of both pipeline and data parallel sections.
Here we want to state all the reasonably possible parallel shapes, not the ones we think are the most performant.
Example using scan:
```
Flows#(1,2,3,4).scan(0, {acc, e -> acc + e}).list
```
returns `Lists#(1,3,6,10)`: the accumulator is threaded and exactly one element comes out per element in.
Here we keep a sum of all the elements seen up to now and we return the current sum.

`scan` is not a primitive, it is an `actor` whose state is a `Var`:

`.scan(acc, f) -> this.actor[mut Var[imm S], S](Vars#[imm S](acc), {down, s, e -> Block#(s.set(f#(s.get, e)), down.push#(down,s.get), down.continue)})`

The sink the lambda pushes to, and what the lambda answers:
```
ActorSink[E:*]: {
  read .push: ActorSinkHelper -> ActorSinkHelper;
  read .continue: ActorContinue -> ActorContinue;
  read .stop: ActorStop -> ActorStop;
  mut #(E): Void;
  mut .pushError(Info): Void;
  }
ActorSinkHelper: Sealed {
  imm #[E:*](down: mutH ActorSink[E], e: E): Void -> _Magic!;//calls down#(e) bypassing the type system
  }
ActorRes: Sealed {}
ActorContinue: Sealed, ActorRes {}
ActorStop: Sealed, ActorRes {}
```
`ActorContinue` and `ActorStop` are imm enumeration elements, not active entities.

`ActorSink.#` is a mut method, so on the mutH sink of `actorMut` it accepts only an `iso` element: every argument of a mut method called on a hygienic receiver is strengthened, mut to iso and read to imm. Declaring that `#` mutH would say exactly what we want and the language does not allow it, so `ActorSinkHelper` takes the sink as an argument of an imm method and reaches `down#e` with magic. `down#e` stays available directly wherever the sink is plain mut, as it is in `actor`.

That same strengthening is what stops the actor from keeping an alias of what it has just sent: the state is mutH, so storing a mut element in it would be a mut method taking a mut argument, which does not typecheck. It is also why the sink itself can not go downstream: it would have to be the element type of another sink, and a mutH value can not be stored anywhere to get there.

**Writing an actorMut lambda:**\
A hygienic parameter can not be captured by a lambda, and every branching method of `Bool` takes one. So inside an `actorMut` lambda a branch can not mention the sink or the state: `b ? {.then -> down.stop; ..}` does not compile. A branch answers with `ActorContinue` or `ActorStop` written out, and a branch that has to push picks an imm step first and hands it the sink as an argument, the same move `ActorSinkHelper` makes:
```
HeroStep: { read #(down: mutH ActorSink[mut Hero], h: mut Hero): ActorRes }
PushHero: HeroStep { down, h -> Block#(down.push#(down, h), ActorContinue) }
DropHero: HeroStep { down, h -> ActorContinue }
```
This is a real limit on what an `actorMut` lambda can say, and it is why `.flatMap` has no `actorMut` shape at all.
In `actor` the sink and the state are ordinary mut values and lambdas may capture them, so `Block#.do{..}` and the ordinary branching methods work there.

