### mapping

`l.flow.map f .mapping(by, kem)`

Let `put` be what the terminal does with one element: `put#(x)` applies `kem.key` and `kem.elem` to `x`, uses `by` to place the key among the keys already present, and throws when the key is already present. Which calls `put` makes is left under-specified, as under `.sort`. If there is a smallest index `j` such that `put#(f#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise this flow returns the map holding one entry per element, in index order.

Two elements carrying the same key make `put` throw at the later of the two indices, so the flow raises rather than keeping one value and dropping the other. In other flow/stream libraries the same chain may keep either value without a word.

